          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.3 Sexual Reproduction in Animals"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.113,
            0.89,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reproduction in animals takes place through asexual and/or sexual method(s). We have studied different methods of asexual reproduction in different organisms, such as budding and spore formation, earlier in this chapter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.038,
            0.212,
            0.207,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.043,
            0.256,
            0.207,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Why do animals with external fertilisation generally produce more eggs than animals with internal fertilisation?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.044,
            0.398,
            0.219,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. In animals, which fertilisation method the gametes are more protected?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.244,
            0.191,
            0.321,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.244,
                0.191,
                0.321,
                0.23
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.24,
            0.185,
            0.89,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Grade 6 In many aquatic animals exhibiting sexual reproduction, Curiosity such as frogs and most fish, fertilisation occurs outside the body. Chapter 10 The female releases eggs into the water, and the male releases sperm over eggs for fertilisation to take place. This method of fertilisation is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "external fertilisation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Although a large number of eggs are laid, many are destroyed by water currents or eaten by other animals. In reptiles, birds and mammals, fertilisation takes place inside the body of the female. This is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "internal fertilisation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Here, the chances of survival of the young ones are generally higher because the fertilised egg or embryo is protected more in internal fertilisation (Table 11.4)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.363,
            0.797,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.4 Variations in Reproduction in Animals"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.241,
            0.387,
            0.89,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Animals show a wide variety of methods of sexual reproduction. However, all animals face the basic challenge of ensuring that the male and the female gametes meet, and that the young ones survive long enough to grow and reproduce. Study the information given in Table 11.4 for four categories of animals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.063,
            0.503,
            0.885,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.242,
                0.483,
                0.79,
                0.5
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 11.4: Variation in reproductive strategies among animals"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.063,
                0.503,
                0.885,
                0.658
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_table_10.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Animal</td><td>Habitat</td><td>Mode of fertilisation</td><td>Number of eggs produced</td><td>Estimated survival of young ones</td></tr><tr><td>Fish</td><td>Water</td><td>External</td><td>100s - 1000s at a time</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Frog</td><td>Water/land</td><td>External</td><td>5,000 - 50,000 at a time</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Lizard</td><td>Land</td><td>Internal</td><td>2 - 20 at a time</td><td>Moderate</td></tr><tr><td>Bird</td><td>Water/land</td><td>Internal</td><td>1 - 15 at a time</td><td>Moderate to High</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.032,
            0.688,
            0.289,
            0.864
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.032,
                0.688,
                0.289,
                0.864
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.044,
                0.87,
                0.285,
                0.887
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 11.17: Life cycle of a butterfly"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.669,
            0.89,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fish, amphibians, and insects produce several hundred to several thousand eggs at a time with yolk, which contains nutritive substances to nourish the developing embryos. The mother’s body cannot provide such large quantities of yolk for so many eggs. The strategy these species adopt is that the yolk in the egg is just enough to produce a larva, which hatches from the egg. The larva, then gets nutrition by eating organic wastes like rotten food, manures and so on, and grows. This stage is an intermediate stage in its development, which is essentially a feeding stage. Once enough nutrition is accumulated, a transformation takes place and the adult body is formed — as seen in organisms, such as butterfly (Fig. 11.17) and frogs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.875,
            0.89,
            0.944
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In contrast, reptiles and birds lay eggs, and each egg contains enough yolk to nourish the developing embryo until it hatches into a young one. In mammals, the zygote grows and develops inside the female body."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
